      "dom_id": "question-1418",
      "name": null,
      "problem": "An adult drinks a cup of coffee that contains $110$ mg of caffeine, and each hour the amount of caffeine in their body decreases by $15%.$ To the nearest integer, how much caffeine will be left in their system after $11$ hours?",
      "worked_solution": "The formula for exponential decay (in terms of a decay rate $r$) is\n\n$$\ny=a(1-r)^{t}.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have the following:\n\n- $a=110$ is the initial amount of caffeine in the adult's body.\n\n- $r=0.15$ is the decay rate ($15%$ expressed as a decimal).\n\n- $t=$ the time, in hours.\n\nTherefore, the correct expression for $y$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =110\\cdot (1-0.15)^{t} \\\\  & =110\\cdot (0.85)^{t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluating the expression for $t=11$ hours, we get\n\n$$\ny(11)=110\\cdot (0.85)^{11}≈18.\n$$\n\nHence, after $11$ hours, there will be $18$ mg of caffeine left in their system.",
