      "dom_id": "question-62052",
      "name": null,
      "problem": "Austin's average monthly profit has been decreasing at a rate of $6%$ every year. If he initially recorded an average monthly profit of $$58\\,000$, then which function correctly represents $p(t),$ Austin's average monthly profit since the start of the decline?",
      "worked_solution": "The formula for exponential decay (in terms of a decay rate $r$) is\n\n$$\np(t)=a(1-r)^{t}.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have the following:\n\n- $a=58\\,000$ is the initial average monthly profit.\n\n- $r=0.06$ is the decay rate ($6%$ expressed as a decimal).\n\n- $t=$ the time, in years.\n\nTherefore, the correct expression for $p$ is\n\n$$\np(t)=58\\,000\\cdot (1-0.06)^{t}=58\\,000\\cdot (0.94)^{t}.\n$$",
