      "dom_id": "question-62061",
      "name": null,
      "problem": "A lake currently has a depth of $320$ feet. The depth of the lake is expected to decrease by one-tenth every year.\n\nThe function $d(t)$ models the depth of the lake after $t$ years and is given by\n\n$$\nd(t)=320\\cdot {(\\text{___})}^{t}.\n$$\n\nWhat number is missing?",
      "worked_solution": "If the depth reduces by $\\frac{1}{10}$ every month, then $\\frac{9}{10}$ of the depth remains every year. So the decay factor is $\\frac{9}{10}.$\n\nThe formula for exponential decay (in terms of a decay factor $b$) is\n\n$$\nd(t)=a{b}^{t}.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have the following:\n\n- $a=320$ is the initial depth.\n\n- $b=\\frac{9}{10}$ is the decay factor.\n\n- $t=$ the time, in years.\n\nTherefore, the correct expression for $d(t)$ is\n\n$$\nd(t)=320\\cdot {(\\frac{9}{10})}^{t}.\n$$\n\nSo the missing number is $\\frac{9}{10}.$",
